<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font color="#ff0000"><font
face="Arial, sans-serif"><font size="4"><strong>&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;</strong></font></font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">When Thomas Stevens was 22, he had a
serious accident. He fell toward a window, and put his arms out to break
the fall. Instead, his left arm went through the glass and he severed most
of its tendons and arteries. His then-girlfriend, Sarah, had the presence
of mind to make a tourniquet out of her blouse, tie it around his arm and
call an ambulance. &ldquo;Oddly,&rdquo; Thomas says, &ldquo;I was somehow
calm during all of this. Even though I heard the EMT telling Sarah that he
didn&rsquo;t think I would make it to the hospital, I still felt well taken
care of.&rdquo;</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Once at the hospital Thomas kissed Sarah
(&ldquo;I did think I might not make it out of the hospital&rdquo;) and was
rushed into Emergency surgery. He had lost so much blood that he could not
have anesthesia so he was in a unique position of watching everything that
went on. It was apparent that the operating team was working feverishly to
reconnect all the arteries and tendons. Something else also held
Tom&rsquo;s attention. &ldquo;During the surgery, a rather small nurse with
dark brown, almost black hair and green eyes held my right hand and just
kind of stood beside me,&rdquo; he says. Apparently, this was the
nurse&rsquo;s only job, that, and offering encouragement to Thomas.
&ldquo;She told me on multiple occasions that I had to fight because there
was so much left for me to do,&rdquo; he recalls.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Once in recovery, Tom&rsquo;s family
gathered, along with the physician who had performed the surgery. He asked
to speak to Tom alone for a moment, so everyone else left the room.
&ldquo;Tom, you lost so much blood that you should have died,&rdquo; the
doctor began. &ldquo;You&rsquo;ve been given a second chance at life now,
and you need to make the most of it.&rdquo; Tom asked for details of the
surgery, and what kind of rehabilitation he would need to regain the use of
his hand. </font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">The doctor looked at Tom sadly.
&ldquo;Son, you&rsquo;re never going to regain any feeling in that
hand---and you&rsquo;re going to need extensive therapy to even be able to
use it.&rdquo; </font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Tom was shocked. He was so young, and this
was a bitter blow. Almost immediately, he thought of the dark-haired nurse
who&rsquo;d held his hand during surgery and spoken so encouragingly to
him. &ldquo;Could I see her?&rdquo; he asked the doctor. </font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">The physician looked puzzled. &ldquo;I
don&rsquo;t know who you&rsquo;re talking about&rdquo; he said to
Tom.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in">&ldquo;<font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Small and dark-haired,
she held my hand&hellip;&rdquo; Tom tried to explain, but the doctor was
shaking his head.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in">&ldquo;<font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">There wasn&rsquo;t any
nurse in the room at all. Just me and the other surgeon.&rdquo; Then Tom
understood. The nurse had been right. There were many more things he had to
do with his life, and God would give him the grace to do it all.
</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Today, Thomas has not only completely
regained the use of his hand, but is married and the father of three small
children. He has also been serving for the past six years in the Air Force,
with no complications to his hand. &ldquo;I do have a scar,&rdquo; he says.
&ldquo;It is about 8 by 6 inches, and is in the form of a cross on the
bottom of my left forearm.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s a daily reminder that God did
give Thomas a second chance---and he plans to make the most of it.
</font></font></p>
